    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the expression \\(\\frac{(N-1)!(N)}{(N+1)!}\\), we will start by expressing the factorial terms in a way that allows us to simplify the fraction.\n\nFirst, recall the definition of factorial. The factorial of a number \\(k\\), denoted \\(k!\\), is the product of all positive integers up to \\(k\\). So, we have:\n\\[\n(N-1)! = (N-1) \\times (N-2) \\times (N-3) \\times \\cdots \\times 2 \\times 1\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n(N+1)! = (N+1) \\times N \\times (N-1) \\times (N-2) \\times (N-3) \\times \\cdots \\times 2 \\times 1.\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute these into the original expression:\n\\[\n\\frac{(N-1)!(N)}{(N+1)!} = \\frac{(N-1) \\times (N-1) \\times (N-2) \\times \\cdots \\times 2 \\times 1 \\times N}{(N+1) \\times N \\times (N-1) \\times (N-2) \\times \\cdots \\times 2 \\times 1}.\n\\]\n\nNotice that the \\((N-1)!\\) and \\((N+1)!\\) terms in the numerator and the denominator cancel each other out, leaving us with:\n\\[\n\\frac{(N-1)!(N)}{(N+1)!} = \\frac{N}{(N+1) \\times N} = \\frac{1}{N+1}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the simplified form of the given expression is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{N+1}}.\n\\]",
