    "text": "To solve the expression \\(\\frac{(N-1)!(N)}{(N+1)!}\\), we will first simplify the factorial terms and then perform the division.\n\n1. **Factorial Simplification:**\n   Recall that \\((N+1)! = (N+1) \\cdot N!\\). Therefore, we can rewrite the denominator as:\n   \\[\n   (N+1)! = (N+1) \\cdot N!\n   \\]\n\n2. **Substitute the Simplified Denominator:**\n   Substitute \\((N+1) \\cdot N!\\) for \\((N+1)!\\) in the original expression:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(N-1)!(N)}{(N+1)!} = \\frac{(N-1)!(N)}{(N+1) \\cdot N!}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Cancel Common Terms:**\n   Notice that \\((N-1)!\\) in the numerator and \\((N-1)!\\) in the denominator cancel each other out:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(N-1)!(N)}{(N+1) \\cdot N!} = \\frac{N}{(N+1) \\cdot N!}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Simplify Further:**\n   The \\(N\\) in the numerator and the \\(N\\) in the denominator cancel each other out:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{N}{(N+1) \\cdot N!} = \\frac{1}{(N+1) \\cdot (N-1)!}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Final Simplification:**\n   The expression \\(\\frac{1}{(N+1) \\cdot (N-1)!}\\) is already in its simplest form.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{(N+1)(N-1)!}}\n\\]",
